Creating an HTML page that displays the background image of the user's computer is possible using JavaScript and the `window` object. Below is an example of how you can achieve this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Display Background Image</title>
</head>
<body>
    <button onclick="displayBackgroundImage()">Show Background Image</button>

    <script>
        function displayBackgroundImage() {
            // Get the current window size (height)
            const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;

            // Use the window's innerHeight property to get the height of the browser viewport.
            const elementWidth = document.body.offsetWidth;

            // Create a new canvas element with dimensions equal to the window size
            const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
            canvas.width = elementWidth;
            canvas.height = windowHeight;

            // Get the context for drawing on the canvas
            const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

            // Draw the canvas into the body tag as the background image
            document.body.appendChild(canvas);

            // Remove the canvas from the DOM after it has been used once
            setTimeout(() => {
                document.body.removeChild(canvas);
            }, 5000); // Delay of 5 seconds before removing the canvas

            // Set the style for the canvas to be hidden
            canvas.style.display = 'none';
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How It Works:
1. **Button**: A button is added to the HTML page that when clicked will call the `displayBackgroundImage()` function.

2. **JavaScript Function**:
   - The `displayBackgroundImage` function creates a new `<canvas>` element and sets its width and height to match the current window size (`innerWidth` and `innerHeight`).
   - It then draws the canvas onto the `body` of the HTML page so that it becomes the background image.
   - After about 5 seconds, the canvas is removed from the `body`, effectively hiding it.

3. **CSS Styling**: By default, the `<canvas>` element is styled to be invisible, making sure it doesn't interfere with other elements on your webpage.

